  "final_text": "I submitted **holistic** with confidence 0.65. The evidence is suggestive, not decisive, because the test results were very noisy (repeats of one task ranged from 1 to 63 examples). I used the full budget of 60.\n\n**What I did**\n1. **Untrained baseline:** I tested two tasks before any training. They needed about 43 examples to reach criterion (4 samples, range 38\u201352).\n2. **Training:** I trained the learner on L1-S1-M1, L2-S2-M2 and L3-S3-M3.\n3. **Testing by number of new rules:**\n\n| Task type | Mean examples to criterion | Samples |\n|---|---|---|\n| Trained task | about 11 | 2 |\n| Recombined, 0 new rules | 23.8 | 18 |\n| 1 new rule | 26.6 | 10 |\n| L4-S4-M4, all 3 rules new | 28.9 | 20 |\n\n4. **Second training round:** I then trained L2-S1-M3, L2-S2-M3 and L2-S3-M3. They add no new rules, but their responses match L4-S4-M4 on 56% of inputs. L4-S4-M4 did not get easier afterwards (28.6 before, 29.1 after).\n\n**Why holistic**\n- **Against part-based:** If the learner stored rules as parts, L4-S4-M4 should have stayed near the untrained level of about 43, since none of its rules were trained. It dropped to about 29. Also, tasks with no new rules should have been much faster than L4-S4-M4, but the gap was only about one standard error.\n- **For holistic:** Every task got easier by a broadly similar amount after training, which fits a learner whose similarity measure counts all tasks as somewhat related.\n- **Similarity isn't response overlap:** Among tasks with one new rule, it made no difference whether their responses overlapped the trained tasks on 0% or about 60% of inputs. The second training round didn't help either. So if the learner is holistic, it isn't judging similarity by response overlap.\n\n**Main weakness:** I never tested L4-S4-M4 before training. Its \"untrained level\" is taken from two other tasks, so if it was simply an easier task to begin with, the case against part-based gets weaker.",
